---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const title =
  "Fidelic: run your Salesforce integration tests without waiting for a sandbox.";
const description =
  "Salesforce integration tests that run on your laptop and in CI — no org, no login, nothing shared. Fidelic is a 10 MB container that loads your real schema and runs your real Apex triggers, so the tests run on every push instead of whenever an environment frees up.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  {/* SECTION 1 — the problem. SANDBOX ELIMINATION IS THE PRODUCT; emulation is HOW it
      works and has been demoted to section 2 accordingly. The old hero led with "no real CI",
      which names a category rather than the thing a reader is currently waiting on.

      EVERY CLAIM ON THIS PAGE TRACES TO A COMMAND, per the standing rule. The table is here so
      the next person editing this copy can re-run them instead of trusting them:

        "without waiting for a sandbox"  → docs/ci/github-actions.md; real workflow run
                                           35263998942, 16s, 4/4 tests, no org involved
        "on every push"                  → same run, triggered by push
        "10 MB"                          → hub.docker.com/r/fidelic/emulator, 10.34 MB compressed
        "starts in under a second"       → docker run … --apex-src ./mdapi → 0.29s to listening
                                           (8 triggers). A 1,500-class org takes ~15s; boot is
                                           proportional to how much Apex you have, and QUICKSTART
                                           §3 says so rather than this page implying otherwise.
        "about two milliseconds"         → POST /__emulator__/reset, median 1.9ms over HTTP, n=20
        "side by side"                   → two containers on different ports: a record created in
                                           one leaves the other at totalSize 0
        "row locks, rate limits"         → POST /__emulator__/faults; conformance faults-001..005
        "your real Apex triggers"        → GET /__emulator__/fired, outcome entered-clean

      FINDINGS #18 review: no count of triggers that RUN appears on this page. The only trigger
      count is in the playground section, which says "loaded and classified" and is unchanged. */}
  <h1>Run your Salesforce integration tests without waiting for a sandbox.</h1>

  <p class="sub">
    Sandboxes are scarce, shared, and slow to refresh. Tests queue for an environment,
    then fail because someone else changed the data underneath them. So the tests that
    would catch the most expensive bugs are the ones that get run least.
  </p>

  {/* SECTION 2 — the mechanism. Emulation is the HOW. */}
  <h2>It runs your org on your own machine, so nothing queues.</h2>

  <p class="answer">
    Fidelic is a 10 MB container that loads your org's real schema and runs your real Apex
    triggers. Your tests point at <code>localhost</code> and get the same REST responses, the
    same trigger side effects, the same validation errors.
  </p>

  {/* The CI claim above must be reachable, not merely asserted: this is the recipe that was
      executed as a real workflow run before it was published. */}
  <p class="answer">
    <a href="/docs/ci-github-actions">Copy the CI recipe</a>. It's a
    GitHub Actions workflow that boots the org, waits until your Apex has actually loaded,
    runs your tests and tears down.
  </p>

  <p class="answer">
    It can also fail on purpose: one call creates a row lock, a rate limit, or slow responses,
    and the next request returns the real Salesforce error. You cannot make a sandbox
    do that. <a href="/capabilities">See the full list of capabilities</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="answer receipts-line">
    Every claim on this page traces to a command. <a href="/receipts">See the receipts</a>.
  </p>

  {/* THE THREE-COMMAND PROOF BLOCK MOVED TO /receipts.
      It ran analyzer, then boot, then an armed fault — three commands deep, sitting between the
      hero copy and everything that explains it. Raw output is what /receipts is for, and the
      people who want to read it are already going there. What stays here is the ONE artifact
      the hero's own claim needs: speed to listening.

      TRANSCRIPT, NOT A COMPOSITION. Both lines are verbatim program output, re-verified against
      fidelic/emulator:4ea9d49 on 2026-09-19, NPSP force-app subtree (26 triggers, 1,503 classes).

      Reproduce:
        docker run -p 8080:8080 -v ./npsp/force-app:/mdapi:ro fidelic/emulator --apex-src /mdapi

      THE FAULT ENV VAR IS GONE FROM THE COMMAND, deliberately: this block no longer demonstrates
      a fault, and a flag whose effect is never shown is noise the reader has to explain away. The
      boot line is identical either way — it reports store, client-secret, metadata and hooks-file,
      none of which faults touch — and that was checked rather than assumed.

      THE BUILD STAMP IS ELIDED ON PURPOSE. The line really reads `sfemu <sha> listening on :8080 …`
      and that sha changes on every release, so writing one here would date this page the moment
      the next image ships — the same trap that left "v1.0" on this line for months. The … is where
      the stamp was; everything either side of it is byte-for-byte what the program printed.

      The 14.1s is OURS, not the program's — sfemu prints no boot duration — so it is marked with a
      # as a comment. Measured on the production box, a Hetzner CPX21 with 3 vCPU. */}
  <div
    class="terminal"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Terminal transcript: the emulator loads the Nonprofit Success Pack from source and is listening on port 8080 in 14.1 seconds."
  >
    <pre><span class="cmd">docker run -p 8080:8080 -v ./npsp/force-app:/mdapi:ro fidelic/emulator --apex-src /mdapi</span>
sfemu … listening on :8080 (store: sqlite ":memory:", client-secret: false, metadata: "/mdapi", hooks-file: "")
<span class="dim"># 14.1s to listening — NPSP, 26 triggers / 1,503 classes, 3-vCPU cloud VM</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p class="answer">
    Before anything runs, every trigger in your org gets one of four labels, so you know
    what you are looking at:
  </p>

  <ul class="answer-list">
    <li>
      <strong>EXECUTE</strong>: nothing in the reachable code is unsupported, so the
      interpreter will run your real Apex.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>HOOKS</strong>: The effect is reproduced by a rule instead of
      by running the Apex.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>RECORDED-ONLY</strong>: the code could run, but the thing that starts it
      cannot happen on your machine such as a platform event, for instance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>UNRESOLVED</strong>: refused! You get the file name, line number, and the construct
      it uses that we do not support.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="answer">
    <a href="/capabilities#vocabulary">See exactly what we support</a>
  </p>

  {/* SECTION 2b — the live playground.
      A LINK AND A REAL TRANSCRIPT, not an iframe. An iframe would load the whole
      playground app into this page for every visitor who never clicks it, which is
      third-party JavaScript in the critical path of a site that currently ships none.
      The transcript below is verbatim output from a real session against the live
      box — not a mock-up — so it stays honest without costing a single byte of image. */}
  <div class="tryit">
    <h2>Or run it now, in your browser.</h2>

    <p class="answer">
      {/* FINDINGS #18: no artifact may state or imply that N triggers RUN. This line
          previously read "26 triggers, 7 of them executing real Apex" — but 7 is the
          CLASSIFIED-EXECUTE count, and runtime verification puts 2 of those 7 entered
          and 5 refused. "Loaded and classified" is what the scanner knows; "one of its
          real handlers fires" is what corpus-exec and the playground's own condition 7
          actually verify. Claim the verified thing. */}
      <a href="https://play.fidelic.dev">play.fidelic.dev</a> runs a real
      <a href="https://github.com/salesforcefoundation/npsp">NPSP </a> org with the actual open-source nonprofit codebase. All 26 triggers in that repo
      are loaded and classified. Click a command and inspect one of its real handlers fire, then
      throw the org away. No install, no signup, no Salesforce login.
    </p>

    <div
      class="terminal"
      role="img"
      aria-label="Playground transcript: creating a DataImportBatch record returns HTTP 201, and the fired-trigger log shows TDTM_DataImportBatch running on both before-insert and after-insert."
    >
      <pre><span class="accent">POST /services/data/v62.0/sobjects/DataImportBatch__c</span> <span class="dim">&#123;"Name":"Spring Appeal"&#125;</span>
<span class="ok">201</span>  <span class="dim">&#123;"id":"a0400000000006zAAA","success":true&#125;</span>

<span class="accent">GET /__emulator__/fired</span>
  <span class="ok">TDTM_DataImportBatch</span>  <span class="dim">before insert</span>  entered-clean
  <span class="ok">TDTM_DataImportBatch</span>  <span class="dim">after  insert</span>  entered-clean</pre>
    </div>

    <p>
      <a class="btn" href="https://play.fidelic.dev">Open the playground →</a>
    </p>

    {/* Says what is true rather than what sells. The pool is small and finite, and a
        visitor who meets the busy path should have been told it exists. */}
    <p class="soon">
      A handful of sandboxes run at a time. If they are all busy you will be given
      the command to run it yourself instead.
    </p>
  </div>

  {/* SECTION 3 — receipts, proof, CTA */}
  <div class="receipts">
    <p>
      Verified 70/70 against the public conformance suite
      <a href="https://github.com/fidelic-dev/fidelic-conformance"
        >run the scorecard yourself →</a
      >
    </p>
    <p class="soon">
      70/70 pass · 5 skipped (need a real org) · 3 pending (awaiting real-org
      verification). The skips and pendings are the methodology showing its
      work.
    </p>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="pullquote">
    <p>
      The failure injection alone is worth it. Row locks and API limits are
      untestable in any real org. Fidelic makes them a config flag.
    </p>
    <cite>— Engineer at a major Salesforce partner</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <div class="waitlist" id="waitlist">
    <h2>Get early access</h2>
    <p>
      We onboard a few teams at a time. Real CI for your Salesforce stack starts
      with an email.
    </p>
    <form action="https://formspree.io/f/mkodpozp" method="POST">
      <input
        type="email"
        name="email"
        placeholder="you@company.com"
        aria-label="Email address"
        required
      />
      <input type="hidden" name="_subject" value="Fidelic early access" />
      <input
        type="hidden"
        name="_next"
        value="https://www.fidelic.dev/thanks"
      />
      <button class="btn" type="submit">Get early access</button>
    </form>
  </div>
</Base>
